15. UNIVERSAL MULTITRACK TIMELINE
========================================================

Track types:
- Main Video
- Secondary Video
- Overlay
- Audio
- Voice-over
- Text
- Subtitle
- Sticker
- Effects
- Adjustment

Use one universal timeline model.

Every newly imported independently positioned visual overlay
must receive its own separate track by default.

Users can long-press and drag an item to another track or location.

If an item is moved into an occupied position within the same
track, use safe ripple/reorder behavior.

Never cause accidental overlap or data loss.

Between adjacent main-video clips:
- Show a visible plus (+) control.
- Keep clips magnetically adjacent.
- Insert a transition only when the user taps the plus.

Timeline interactions:
- Two-finger pinch zoom.
- Fine-grained zoom.
- Zoom anchor preservation.
- High-speed horizontal flick.
- Inertial scrolling.
- Reverse flick.
- Repeated fast flick.
- Touch interruption of inertia.
- Start/end navigation.
- Overview navigator.
- Minimap.
- Previous/next clip.
- Previous/next marker.

The playhead remains fixed at the zero-second/start screen
position while timeline content scrolls beneath it.

Selected clip:
- Show left and right trim handles.
- Use visible white trim handles.
- Keep playhead inside selected clip boundaries while
  selected-clip confinement is active.
- Clear selection restores project-level navigation.

Main video and audio remain in the same timeline model.
Audio may have a focused detailed view, but it must not
become an independent timeline.

========================================================
16. TIMELINE TIMEBASE AND MEDIA ACCURACY
========================================================

Use:
- 64-bit microseconds as canonical timebase.
- Rational frame-rate representation.
- Correct frame-boundary calculations.
- Variable Frame Rate timestamp mapping where available.
- Overflow-safe arithmetic.

Do not use floating-point seconds as the canonical timeline model.

Source time and timeline time must remain distinct.

Main/source video trimming:
- Cannot extend beyond the real source duration.
- Cannot fabricate source frames.
- Cannot append blank or repeated tails.
- Must preserve valid source-in/source-out boundaries.

Non-video items may have item-specific duration behavior,
such as hold, loop or visibility duration.

Never silently append even one frame or second beyond
the actual project end.

Export duration must match the intended timeline end under
the defined frame/timebase rounding policy.

Validate after:
- Speed changes
- Ripple edits
- Undo/Redo
- Save/Load
- Preview
- Export

